In article <HAIRSTON.90Jul11214652@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>[cos@chaos.cs.brandeis.edu (Ofer Inbar) writes:]
>[] Something occured to me a few minutes ago, while I was playing NetHack
>[] 3.0.9.  My +1 shield rusted down to -2.  Now, I wonder, how badly can
>[] a shield rust, that wearing it makes your AC worse than it was without
>[] the shield.
>
>sometimes it helps not to be too literal about the workings of the
>dungeon.  i like to think that the rust reduced the "magic" enchantment
>of the armor, in which case it is perfectly okay for an armor with a
>negative magic enchantment to be worse than no armor at all.  what say
>you?

What say I?  I say that I don't like to think that getting something
wet gives that object a magical negative, even though it looks like it
merely rusted.  I think having it crumble away is a better idea.
It's always easy to rationalize away illogical quirks.  If a
gelatinous cube could weild a two-handed sword, you'd probably find a
good explanation for that, too.  But in this case, the fix is so
obvious and trivial that I see no reason to object.  It's just a
suggestion to the dev team; I'm hoping that they will incorporate it
into a future release of the game.

BTW, I like your method of giving hints.  Keep it up.

